10:04.679 --> 10:05.679
one more time.

10:05.679 --> 10:11.700
So when you're thinking about using subagents as the way to add functionality versus things

10:11.700 --> 10:17.000
like skills and commands, remember first of all, it allows you to do more in parallel

10:17.000 --> 10:18.000
at the same time.

10:18.000 --> 10:20.720
You can have multiple subagents working together.

10:20.720 --> 10:22.320
They can be self-corrective.

10:22.320 --> 10:24.639
They can not only allow you to do more at once,

10:24.639 --> 10:26.679
but they can also sort of have a negative feedback

10:26.679 --> 10:28.399
that they keep you more under control,

10:28.399 --> 10:29.639
doing a bit less.

10:29.639 --> 10:31.840
They can be very efficient for your context.

10:31.840 --> 10:34.320
It's usually the first reason you use subagents,

10:34.320 --> 10:37.039
is 'cause you pull stuff off the main context

10:37.039 --> 10:40.320
for Cloud Code, and they can be focused on one task

10:40.320 --> 10:52.000
that allows you to perfect the prompting, perfect the task at hand, do one thing really well and be really good at it, keeping again that kind of context of your main Claude code.

10:52.000 --> 10:55.000
But with these strengths come a few drawbacks.

10:55.000 --> 11:04.980
So any time you've got this kind of ability to delegate to some other process, you're adding more moving parts. There's more complexity in your system, which

11:04.980 --> 11:09.840
means that more things can go wrong in a mysterious way that's harder to track down.

11:09.840 --> 11:14.820
There are what are called boundary issues or problems at the interfaces between your

11:14.820 --> 11:20.820
sub-agent and your main agent that can result in mistakes. You can have this amplification

11:20.820 --> 11:26.720
of problems when you divvy up the task. Something can go a bit wrong and then agents go off in different directions

11:26.720 --> 11:32.480
and it gets more and more wrong in a way that's kind of hard to recover compounding errors.

11:32.480 --> 11:37.840
And then also whilst subagents are sometimes touted as a way to save money because you can use a

11:37.840 --> 11:43.759
cheaper model to do some of your processing, they can also be a way that money can add up because

11:43.759 --> 11:48.480
you're just doing a lot more. You've got more churn, particularly if you've got the sort of self-correcting

11:48.480 --> 11:53.320
agents that are doing code reviews, doing document reviews, coming back for feedback.

11:53.320 --> 11:58.279
So more is going on. Hopefully that results in better outcomes, but it might also mean

11:58.279 --> 12:01.080
that ultimately you spend more getting there.

